## 🔧 Development Story

Originally, I wanted to build SCOUT as a **full working reconnaissance rover**, with the tracks, motors, sonar and software all working together.

However, because of the time constraints of the Maker Challenge, I realised that trying to finish the entire robot would mean rushing everything. Instead, I broke the project down and focused on three main areas:

- 🛰️ **Sonar/radar system**
- 🛞 **Tracks**
- 💻 **Software**

The **sonar and software were the parts that went best**. I built the ultrasonic sonar system using the HC-SR04 and SG90 servo, then connected it to the Raspberry Pi Pico W and created the web interface to display the readings. This was also my first time working with a lot of the equipment, machines and materials involved in the project, so getting the sonar working was a major achievement for me.

The tracks were a different story. We ran into a problem because some of the parts that had been printed were designed for a different motor than the one we actually needed. Because of the time constraints, we couldn't properly get the motors and tracks working together. Instead, we **makeshifted the track system** so I could still explore the mechanical side of the rover.

In the end, I didn't get a completely working rover, but I did get the **sonar, software and radar system working**, while also designing and building the mechanical parts of SCOUT.

This was a **solo entry**, and it was also my first time working with many of the machines, materials and processes used during the Maker Challenge. Rather than trying to hide what didn't work, I wanted to document it as part of the project.

The biggest thing I learnt was that a project doesn't have to be completely finished to be successful. When I realised I didn't have enough time to build everything, I changed the scope and focused on getting one part — the sonar system — working properly.
